<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  DEBUGGING ONLY. This file must never reach a build that goes to the notary
  service: `get-task-allow` is on Apple's rejection list by name, and a
  submission carrying it comes back Invalid with "The signature includes
  get-task-allow entitlement".

  It exists because the hardened runtime (`codesign --options runtime`, which
  build.sh now always applies) is what stops any process from attaching to this
  one — lldb included. Without this entitlement, `lldb -p $(pgrep Zonas)` answers
  with a permission error and no explanation.

  Opt in explicitly, one build at a time:

      ZONAS_DEBUG_ENTITLEMENTS=1 ./build.sh -r

  release.sh refuses to publish anything whose signature carries entitlements,
  so an accident here fails loudly and locally instead of two minutes into an
  upload.

  Nothing else belongs in this file. Zonas ships with ZERO entitlements: moving
  other applications' windows is governed by TCC —the Accessibility toggle the
  user flips— and not by any entitlement. There is no accessibility entitlement
  for third-party apps, whatever the top Google result for "hardened runtime
  accessibility" says, and `NSAccessibilityUsageDescription` is not a real key
  either. The myth survives because `codesign` embeds invented keys without
  complaining, so nothing ever tells you it did nothing.

  The comment sits AFTER the DOCTYPE for a reason. codesign does not parse
  entitlements with the normal plist parser but with AMFI's, and a comment
  before the DOCTYPE makes it fail with `Failed to parse entitlements:
  AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error near line 8` — while `plutil -lint` calls the
  very same file OK.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.get-task-allow</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
